(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-exam-1-medical-literature-master-exam-form-a"]={"title":"Medical Literature Master Exam — Form A","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"In what context should abnormality be determined?","choices":["Whatever differs from the patient's previous result","Whatever falls outside two standard deviations","Whatever the laboratory reports as high or low","Values known to be associated with disease, disability or death"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, together with a clinically recognizable difference from healthy, rather than by statistical position alone. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"What does a receiver operating characteristic curve plot?","choices":["Sensitivity against disease severity","Positive predictive value against prevalence","Pre-test probability against post-test probability","True positive rate against false positive rate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, with one minus specificity on the horizontal axis, which lets clinicians compare alternative tests for the same diagnosis. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What is overdiagnosis?","choices":["Diagnosing disease at an earlier stage","Ordering more tests than are affordable","Diagnosing the same disease twice","Finding disease that does not help the patient"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The finding brings no benefit to the patient, so the label and any treatment can only add harm. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What does a likelihood ratio below 0.1 indicate?","choices":["Strong evidence to rule out","An indeterminate result","Moderate evidence to rule out","Weak evidence to rule out"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and such a ratio virtually rules out the chance that the patient has the disease, which is the mirror of a ratio above 10 ruling it in. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What distinguishes quasi-experimental from experimental research?","choices":["The researcher controls the treatment but not the assignment","The researcher controls the assignment but not the treatment","The researcher controls both, but the study is smaller","The researcher controls neither treatment nor assignment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and that is why it has less control than a true experiment: without control of assignment, the groups may differ in ways the treatment did not create. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What characterizes a state list of reportable diseases?","choices":["It is identical in every state","It is limited to sexually transmitted infections","It is limited to diseases that can be prevented by vaccination","It is long and varied, spanning infections, poisonings and cancers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A single state list runs from acquired immunodeficiency syndrome and anthrax through arsenic and carbon monoxide poisoning to cancer and congenital anomalies. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A drug lowers blood pressure far more in younger than in older adults. What does age represent here?","choices":["A confounder","A matched control","A source of recall bias","An effect modifier"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the effect of the exposure differs according to the level of another variable, which is effect modification and not a bias. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What do most treatment hypotheses need before their usefulness is accepted?","choices":["Approval from the treating clinician","Testing of usefulness and outcomes","A plausible mechanism of disease","Use in a large number of patients"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because most hypotheses, however they arise, require testing to evaluate how useful the treatment is and what outcomes it produces. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"If early treatment after screening is not effective, how does screening affect the time of death?","choices":["It makes death impossible to date","It does not change it","It delays death","It brings death forward"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Diagnosis comes earlier but death occurs at the same time, so survival after diagnosis looks longer without anyone living longer. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which is an advantage of a meta-analysis?","choices":["A stronger, more precise estimate of effect size","Immunity to flaws in the included studies","Freedom from publication bias","No need for inclusion and exclusion criteria"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because combining similar studies narrows the uncertainty around the effect compared with any one of them alone. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What does a clinical prediction rule estimate?","choices":["The prevalence of disease in a community","Outcome probability from patient characteristics","The best treatment from a list of drugs","The sensitivity of a test in a new setting"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, using a set of characteristics rather than one finding, which predicts outcome better than any single data point. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Researchers hope to show that a new drug lowers blood pressure more than placebo. What is the null hypothesis?","choices":["The drug lowers pressure in every patient","The placebo lowers pressure more than the drug","The drug and placebo lower pressure equally","The drug lowers pressure more than placebo"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the null hypothesis is that there is no difference, which is the opposite of what the researchers hope to find. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which is a question to ask when appraising a prognosis study?","choices":["Were outcomes assessed objectively and blindly?","Was the study limited to a single center?","Were the participants free of the disease?","Was the exposure assigned by the investigator?"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the others are whether an inception cohort was assembled, whether entry criteria were objective, whether follow-up was adequate, whether the patients resemble yours and whether important prognostic factors were adjusted for. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which two questions should a provider ask when presented with a study?","choices":["Who funded the study, and were the results statistically significant?","Are the conclusions valid for the sample, and does the sample represent my patients?","How large was the sample, and how long did the study run?","Was the study published in a high-impact journal, and how often is it cited?"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the two map onto the two kinds of validity: the first is internal validity, the second external validity, which is why both have to be asked. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What does the specificity criterion state?","choices":["Less exposure leads to less disease","One effect can have many causes","One cause leads to one effect","Larger exposure leads to more disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, though it is a demanding criterion, because a single exposure often produces several effects and a cause need not be specific to be real. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which database is maintained by the United States National Library of Medicine?","choices":["The Cochrane Library","Medline and PubMed","Clinical Key","Up-To-Date"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it sits alongside the Cochrane Library, Evidence Based Medicine Reviews and Up-To-Date among the named evidence-based resources. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What is a longitudinal ecologic survey?","choices":["Ongoing surveillance or repeated surveys measuring trends","A single survey of a population at one moment","A trial in which exposure is assigned and followed","A study comparing cases with controls for past exposure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it may reveal an association, such as whether lung cancer incidence rises as smoking increases among women over time. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Why should publication bias always be considered when reading a review?","choices":["A random effects model corrects it","Missing studies can distort the evidence","Two reviewers selecting studies removes it","It affects only narrative reviews"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because a review can only include what has been published, so unpublished null results leave it looking at a skewed set of studies. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"How many of the four possible pairings of study conclusion and reality are errors?","choices":["Four","Three","Two","One"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: one error is concluding the treatments differ when they do not, and the other is concluding they do not differ when they do. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"How can a long latency between exposure and disease affect the results of a study?","choices":["It makes the association easier to see","It strengthens the association","It has no effect on the results","It can obscure the association"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because the disease appears long after the exposure, so the two can look unrelated unless people are followed for a sufficient period of time. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What advantage does a retrospective cohort study have over a prospective one?","choices":["It measures exposure in a standardized way","It can examine any patient characteristic","It studies a wider range of risk factors","It is faster, cheaper and more efficient"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because the data were already collected for another purpose and the outcomes have already occurred; the trade-off is a narrower range of risk factors. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What three sources can explain variation within one patient?","choices":["Biologic variation, measurement error, and real disease change","Incidence, prevalence and case fatality","Bias, chance and confounding","Sampling, blinding and randomization"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and identifying which is operating is the clinical point: intervention should only be considered if there is a true pathological change. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"How are the studies in a systematic review summarized?","choices":["Scatter plots and life tables","Funnel plots and dose-response curves","Forest plots and tables of characteristics","Nomograms and receiver operating characteristic curves"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because figures such as forest plots show results across studies, and tables set out the characteristics of each one. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What is incidence density useful for?","choices":["Populations of fixed size followed together","Measuring how many people have a condition now","Dynamic populations, with people moving in and out","Comparing mortality between nations"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which is why it is often used in clinical trials, where participants enroll and leave at different times rather than all being present throughout. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which challenge arises when two sound studies disagree?","choices":["Measurement bias","Outdated evidence","Publication bias","Conflicting evidence"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it sits alongside evidence being simply unavailable or outdated, so the clinician may face too little evidence, too old, or too contradictory. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What can a case-control study be used for?","choices":["Generating hypotheses only","Establishing causation definitively","Generating or testing hypotheses","Measuring the effect of an assigned intervention"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which sets it apart from the cross-sectional design: that one generates hypotheses only, while a case-control study can also test them. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Why does pooling data across studies usually help?","choices":["It converts observational studies into experiments","It removes any bias present in the individual studies","A greater number of participants usually means greater power","It guarantees the result will be statistically significant"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the qualifier usually is deliberate: pooling poor studies produces a larger poor study rather than a reliable answer. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Why does surveillance need to establish a baseline?","choices":["Because a baseline proves the cause of the disease","Because surveillance is required to be passive","Because baselines determine the case definition","Without it a rise in cases cannot be recognized"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and establishing that baseline is listed among its core purposes, alongside determining the magnitude of a health problem. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Why does the level of measurement matter in medical research?","choices":["It determines the prevalence of the condition","It determines whether the study is primary or secondary","It determines whether the measurement is reliable","It determines what can legitimately be said about two values"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: nominal categories can only be counted, ordinal values can be ranked, and only interval data supports statements about how much two values differ. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Why does an age-adjusted death rate differ from a crude one?","choices":["Adjustment removes deaths from unrelated causes","Adjustment changes the multiplier used","Adjustment removes the effect of the population's age structure","Adjustment converts the rate into a proportion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the difference can be substantial: a crude United States rate of about 794 per 100,000 in 2009 corresponded to an age-adjusted figure of about 741. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which two grades carry the same instruction to offer or provide the service?","choices":["C and D","A and B","B and C","D and I"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because both rest on a net benefit judged at least moderate. Grade C offers selectively, Grade D discourages, and Grade I calls for the clinical considerations to be read. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Serious bleeding occurs in 3.0 percent of treated patients and 1.0 percent of control patients. What is the number needed to harm?","choices":["33 patients","50 patients","2 patients","100 patients"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The absolute increase in risk is 3.0 minus 1.0, or 0.02, and one divided by 0.02 is 50. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What evidence supports a 1C recommendation?","choices":["Observational studies, unsystematic experience or flawed trials","Overwhelming evidence from several trial designs","Consistent evidence from well-performed randomized trials","Randomized trials with important limitations"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and any estimate of effect from such evidence is uncertain, although benefits still appear to outweigh risks and burdens. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What happens to the pool of eligible patients as more inclusion and exclusion criteria are applied?","choices":["It shifts entirely into the control group","It shrinks, sometimes to a small fraction","It grows as the population becomes better defined","It stays the same because criteria only describe"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: each set of criteria removes more patients, so the final eligible group can be a small fraction of all patients with the condition. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What is procedural bias?","choices":["Respondents differ systematically from non-respondents","Participants drop out before the study is complete","Interview or questionnaire results are affected by the method of delivery","Care differs between the groups in ways other than the intervention"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the example is pointed: administering a questionnaire after an operation while the patient is still under the after-effects of anesthesia. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What defines a sufficient cause?","choices":["If it is present, the disease sometimes occurs","If it is absent, the disease cannot occur","If it is absent, the disease occurs less often","If it is present, the disease always occurs"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: a sufficient cause precedes the disease and guarantees it whenever present. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"For an intervention that prevents disease, what relative risk is expected?","choices":["A negative number","Greater than one","Less than one","Exactly one"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because prevention should leave the exposed group, those receiving the intervention, with lower risk than the unexposed group. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"The hazard ratio from a Cox analysis is interpreted like which measure?","choices":["Relative risk (risk ratio)","Positive predictive value","Sensitivity","Prevalence"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: a hazard ratio above 1 means more risk of the outcome than the comparison group, and below 1 means less. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which question should be asked about a study of a new diagnostic test?","choices":["Was the study published in a high-impact journal?","Was the test developed commercially?","Did every subject undergo both tests?","How many authors contributed to the study?"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, alongside whether the study included a spectrum of patients and whether those conducting each test were blinded to the other's result. That means the new test and the gold standard. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Why are rates used rather than raw counts?","choices":["Rates remove the effect of chance","Rates establish the cause of a disease","Rates are easier to collect than counts","Rates allow comparisons"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Two places with different populations cannot be compared on counts alone, which is why a denominator and a time period are built into the measure. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which are given as examples of secondary research?","choices":["Meta-analyses and systematic review articles","Laboratory test results and vital signs","Questionnaires and surveys","Original research articles"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because both analyze data that already exists rather than collecting new data. Original research articles are the example of primary research. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Written in the lettered cells, what is positive predictive value?","choices":["a over a plus c","a over a plus b","d over b plus d","d over c plus d"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, dividing true positives by everyone who tested positive, which is why the mix of diseased and healthy people in the population affects it. — Assigned reading: The Classic 2 X 2 Table"},{"q":"What is statistical power?","choices":["The chance of detecting a real difference","The chance of reporting a false difference","The chance that the null hypothesis is true","The size of the difference between groups"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the probability that a study will find a statistically significant difference when the difference really exists. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"In a case-control study, 60 of 100 cases and 30 of 100 controls had been exposed. What is the odds ratio?","choices":["1.5","3.5","0.29","2.0"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the odds of exposure are 60 to 40, which is 1.5, in cases and 30 to 70, about 0.43, in controls, and the ratio is 3.5, matching ad over bc, 4,200 over 1,200. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What is the median?","choices":["The middle score, with half the values on each side","The most frequently occurring value","The average of all the values","The range between the 25th and 75th percentiles"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and because it depends on position rather than magnitude, an extreme value at one end moves it far less than it moves the mean. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"What is compliance in a trial?","choices":["Agreeing to be randomized to a group","Remaining unaware of the assigned treatment","Having the disease being studied","Acting in accordance with instructions"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: it is the degree to which participants act in accordance with instructions, or patients with medical advice. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What is said about the relative strength of study designs?","choices":["Observational designs cannot guide practice at all","All designs carry equal evidential weight","Some are stronger, but all serve a purpose and can guide practice","Only randomized controlled trials should influence practice"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the distinction drawn is between designs that establish cause and effect and those that establish relationships or predictability. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"With what should the outcome of a new treatment be compared?","choices":["The natural, untreated course of the disease","The course in people without the disease","The course in patients who were cured","The cost of the most expensive alternative"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because only against the untreated outcome can a treatment's effect on prognosis be judged. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What defines interval data?","choices":["Categories with no order","Order without defined intervals","Values restricted to two categories","Order with defined, equal intervals"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it splits into two subtypes: continuous data, which can take any value in a range, and discrete data, which takes only specific values. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What is positive predictive value?","choices":["The probability that someone testing negative is disease free","The probability that someone testing positive has the disease","The probability that someone with disease tests positive","The probability that someone without disease tests negative"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, calculated as true positives over true positives plus false positives, which reads across the test-positive row. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is true of studies at the higher levels of an evidence hierarchy?","choices":["They are least vulnerable to bias and more generalizable","They are published more quickly than lower-level studies","They involve fewer participants and so are more precise","They are always experimental rather than observational"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and there is a third property: outcomes are more likely to be attributable to the construct actually being studied rather than to something else. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What is the goal of epidemiologic and clinical research design?","choices":["An unbiased comparison between groups with and without the factor","Proving that the factor causes the outcome in every case","Enrolling as many participants as possible","Describing one group of patients in complete detail"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and everything a good design permits follows from that aim: quantifying the risk difference, determining temporal sequence and minimizing bias. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is the attrition effect, and what is its consequence?","choices":["Survey respondents differ systematically from those who do not respond","Participants drop out of the study, which reduces the sample size","Participants are allocated to groups that differ at baseline","Investigators withhold a subset of their data"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and a smaller sample is the immediate consequence, which matters because a study's ability to detect a real difference depends on how many participants remain. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which study design is the gold standard for evaluating treatment efficacy?","choices":["Cross-sectional survey","Retrospective case-control study","Randomized controlled trial","Prospective cohort study"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because random assignment limits systematic differences between the groups other than the treatment factor, which limits cohort studies. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What does a confidence interval indicate about an estimate?","choices":["Its freedom from bias","Its statistical power","Its clinical importance","Its statistical precision"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: a narrow interval means a precise estimate and a wide interval a less precise one. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which of these is one of the four types of clinical prevention?","choices":["Chemoprevention","Emergency stabilization","Palliative care","Surgical resection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The four types are immunization, screening, behavioral or lifestyle counseling, and chemoprevention, all of which are built into routine practice. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What does endemic mean?","choices":["A concentration of new cases in a given time","A disease that has been eradicated","Disease limited by geographic location","Widespread cases across many regions"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it belongs to distribution by place, alongside the outbreak lists, travel notices and food safety recalls maintained nationally. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which question belongs to quick reading of an abstract?","choices":["What impact will the results have?","Are the conclusions valid?","What limitations existed and were they addressed?","How big was the effect?"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, along with what the study is about, who it applies to, and whether it can and should be applied to your patients. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Why can five-year survival not evaluate a therapy introduced two years ago?","choices":["Five-year survival applies only to screening","Newer therapies never improve survival","No one has yet been followed for five years","The natural history has been changed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: patients treated with a therapy introduced within the past five years, or with a disease duration under five years, cannot be evaluated. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"How can a program reduce the problem of false positives from low prevalence?","choices":["Use a test with lower specificity","Screen higher-risk populations","Stop calculating predictive values","Screen only people without risk factors"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Prevalence is higher in higher-risk groups, so a larger share of positive results are true. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"}]};
